                <pb facs="0317" n="251" sameAs="frus:frus1934v05/pg251"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">816.01/400: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Guatemala</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lawton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-01-24T16:00:00-06:00">January 24, 1934—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:05 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. Foreign Office informs me that everything is arranged for recognition of
                    Martínez by the three republics tomorrow morning. The Guatemalan Foreign
                    Minister will telegraph the Salvadoran Foreign Minister that recognition is
                    accorded and the Foreign Office will at the same time give out a statement to
                    the press. This last will point out that the 1923 treaty has been denounced by
                    Costa Rica and Salvador but that it remains in force for Guatemala, Honduras and
                    Nicaragua. President Ubico himself will make no statement.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Central American Missions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lawton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
